Z||000034||84Bill||06-28-2001||07:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B>.<BR>Hey Flash - this position ensures that, if I hit anything (yeah, right) my a$$ will absorb the first impact.  In theory, at least.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ken we need your input on this theory? <BR>Since Voytek is using his tukas as an airbag, I have some engineering questions that only you can provide.<P>1 what is the maximum g a tukas can take?<BR>2 how will the load on a tukas be propagated throughout the rest of the body?<BR>3 Since a tukas has a natural crumple zone (between the left and right sides) would it be adversly effected in any way by a low speed impact requiring equipment to realign it?<BR>4 Would a tukas qualify as a "passive" restraint or an "active" restraint system or any combination of the two?<BR>5 Should there be a passanger side tukas?<P>Ken I know theese are some tough questions butt we all want to know.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
